{"birds": [{"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 80, "first": "8:37 AM", "last": "10:50 AM", "ts": 1793713031, "mins": [517, 518, 518, 518, 519, 519, 519, 520, 520, 521, 521, 522, 523, 523, 524, 524, 525, 525, 526, 526, 527, 527, 528, 528, 529, 529, 530, 530, 532, 532, 533, 535, 535, 535, 537, 538, 539, 540, 540, 540, 541, 542, 542, 543, 543, 543, 544, 544, 547, 548, 548, 558, 559, 564, 566, 566, 567, 567, 568, 568, 572, 574, 576, 576, 577, 578, 583, 585, 585, 585, 589, 589, 590, 597, 600, 605, 605, 605, 648, 650], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Eastern Bluebird", "sci": "Sialia sialis", "n": 35, "first": "8:37 AM", "last": "11:06 AM", "ts": 1793713035, "mins": [517, 518, 518, 520, 521, 521, 522, 522, 523, 524, 524, 526, 528, 533, 533, 533, 538, 543, 543, 547, 577, 579, 597, 598, 598, 598, 604, 607, 607, 608, 614, 615, 621, 638, 666], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Bluebirds nest in tree cavities, and their numbers crashed in the early 1900s when starlings and house sparrows took the holes. Volunteer nest-box trails helped them rebound.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113046", "snd": "sounds/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113130", "sndby": "Robin Carter, xeno-canto XC1374, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "bluebird", "cm": 18.0, "img": "cutouts/bluebird.png", "w": 800, "h": 609, "len": 744.0, "beak": [0.69, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 24, "b": "AAPwAAAH/AAAD/4AAA/4AAAf8AAAH/AAAD/wAAB/+AAAf/gDAP/wDgH/8BwD//B4A//h4Af/54AH/94AD/98AB//4AA/P4AAPH0AAHPhAABvgAAA3gAAAPgAAABwAAAA"}}, {"name": "Northern Cardinal", "sci": "Cardinalis cardinalis", "n": 18, "first": "8:37 AM", "last": "9:50 AM", "ts": 1793713067, "mins": [517, 521, 528, 548, 548, 549, 549, 550, 552, 570, 570, 571, 571, 572, 572, 589, 590, 590], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Female cardinals sing too, which is unusual for a North American songbird. During courtship the male feeds her seeds beak to beak.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793112978", "snd": "sounds/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793113085", "sndby": "David Tattersley, xeno-canto XC1000248, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "cardinal", "cm": 22.0, "img": "cutouts/cardinal.png", "w": 516, "h": 348, "len": 530.0, "beak": [0.06, 0.28, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AYAAAAeAAAAPgAAAH4AAAB/AAAA/4AAAf/gAAB/+AAAf/4AAH//AAA//8AAP//gAB//8AAf//AAD//+AAf//wEB/v/jwf3v+///+/z//vn945gA+MAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "White-throated Sparrow", "sci": "Zonotrichia albicollis", "n": 5, "first": "8:39 AM", "last": "9:40 AM", "ts": 1793713159, "mins": [519, 527, 563, 574, 580], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "White-throats come in two color forms, white-striped and tan-striped, and almost always pair with the opposite form. Listen for “Oh-sweet-Canada, Canada, Canada.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113071", "snd": "sounds/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113140", "sndby": "Matt Wistrand, xeno-canto XC339648, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "whitethroated", "cm": 17.0, "img": "cutouts/whitethroated.png", "w": 800, "h": 460, "len": 599.0, "beak": [0.73, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 18, "b": "AAB+AAAB/gAAB/8DAB/+BwB//h4B//54f//84P///8AAP/8AAA/8AAAD8AAAA/AAAA8AAAA/AAAAeAAAAeAAAAeAAAAHAAAA"}}, {"name": "Fish Crow", "sci": "Corvus ossifragus", "n": 14, "first": "8:39 AM", "last": "9:17 AM", "ts": 1793713188, "mins": [519, 520, 520, 521, 521, 526, 529, 530, 545, 546, 546, 556, 557, 557], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It looks just like an American Crow. The giveaway is the voice: a nasal, two-note “uh-uh” instead of a clear “caw.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793112991", "snd": "sounds/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793113105", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC130581, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0", "key": "fishcrow", "cm": 38.0, "img": "cutouts/fishcrow.png", "w": 800, "h": 474, "len": 557.0, "beak": [0.27, 0.08, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 19, "b": "AH4AAAD+AAAAPwAAAB+AAAAf4AAAH/AAAB/4AAAf/gAAD/4AAAf/AAAD/4AAAf/AAAD34P8Bs+D///Dg/////v////8AD///AAAP/w=="}}, {"name": "Ruby-crowned Kinglet", "sci": "Corthylio calendula", "n": 1, "first": "8:45 AM", "last": "8:45 AM", "ts": 1793713505, "mins": [525], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its ruby crown stays hidden under olive feathers until the male gets excited, then it flares up. A ruby-crowned kinglet flicks its wings constantly.", "vox": "sounds/voice/corthylio-calendula.mp3?v=1793112988", "snd": "sounds/corthylio-calendula.mp3?v=1793113143", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC104679, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "rubykinglet", "cm": 10.8, "img": "cutouts/rubykinglet.png", "w": 800, "h": 653, "len": 568.0, "beak": [0.09, 0.35, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 26, "b": "AAAAAwAAAAcAAAAGAAAADAAAADgAAAAwB8AAYA/wAOA//ADAH/8BwAf/hwAH/+YAB//8AAP//gAD//+AAf/jAAD/wAAAH4AAAD8AAAB+AAAA8AAAAfAAAA+AAAAeAAAAeAAAAPAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Carolina Wren", "sci": "Thryothorus ludovicianus", "n": 14, "first": "8:46 AM", "last": "9:37 AM", "ts": 1793713560, "mins": [526, 545, 545, 546, 546, 547, 547, 572, 572, 572, 573, 573, 573, 577], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its song sounds like “teakettle, teakettle, teakettle,” and pairs often sing duets. It will nest in odd places: flowerpots, mailboxes, even old boots.", "vox": "sounds/voice/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113060", "snd": "sounds/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113075", "sndby": "Jacob Saucier, xeno-canto XC498646, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "wren", "cm": 13.0, "img": "cutouts/wren.png", "w": 800, "h": 633, "len": 781.0, "beak": [0.85, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 25, "b": "OAAAADgAAAA8AAAAHgB+AB4B/+APA//wB5//AAf//gAH//4AB//+AAf//AAH//wAD//4AAP/+AAB//H/AP/f/wA///8AGf/cAB/7AA//8AAP/4AAP/8AAP9gAAD8AAAAcAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "American Goldfinch", "sci": "Spinus tristis", "n": 47, "first": "8:46 AM", "last": "11:24 AM", "ts": 1793713585, "mins": [526, 526, 529, 529, 530, 530, 533, 535, 535, 536, 536, 537, 537, 538, 538, 539, 540, 540, 541, 541, 541, 542, 542, 543, 543, 544, 544, 544, 546, 547, 548, 548, 549, 550, 550, 551, 569, 569, 569, 570, 577, 577, 589, 602, 607, 610, 684], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "One of the strictest vegetarians among birds. Goldfinches nest late in summer, when thistle seeds are ripe, and swap bright yellow for olive-brown in winter.", "vox": "sounds/voice/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113053", "snd": "sounds/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113090", "sndby": "Will Sweet, xeno-canto XC453934, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "goldfinch", "cm": 12.0, "img": "cutouts/goldfinch.png", "w": 517, "h": 369, "len": 415.0, "beak": [0.18, 0.15, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "APgAAAH4AAAD/AAAB/wAAAP+AAEB/wAHAf+ADgH/wBwB/8DwAf/h4AH/94AA//8AAH/8ABA/+AAAH/wAAB/+AAA/fhAAeA8AA/gHgAfgB8APAANAPAAAADgAAAA="}}, {"name": "White-breasted Nuthatch", "sci": "Sitta carolinensis", "n": 6, "first": "8:47 AM", "last": "10:04 AM", "ts": 1793713637, "mins": [527, 602, 602, 603, 603, 604], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "Nuthatches climb down tree trunks head first. The name comes from “nut-hack”: they wedge a seed into bark and hack it open.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113050", "snd": "sounds/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113095", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC14860, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "nuthatch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/nuthatch.png", "w": 532, "h": 327, "len": 372.0, "beak": [0.79, 0.42, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 20, "b": "fgAAAH8AAAB/AAAAP4AAAD+AAAAfwAAAH/4gAB///gAf///AB///gAf//gAD//wAA//4AAP/8AAB//AAAf/AAAD+AAAA/gAAAP4AAAB/AAA="}}, {"name": "Carolina Chickadee", "sci": "Poecile carolinensis", "n": 3, "first": "8:48 AM", "last": "9:28 AM", "ts": 1793713725, "mins": [528, 535, 568], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Chickadees hide seeds in many separate spots and can remember where for weeks, then dig them back out when food gets scarce.", "vox": "sounds/voice/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113028", "snd": "sounds/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113078", "sndby": "J.R. Rigby, xeno-canto XC309925, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "chickadee", "cm": 12.5, "img": "cutouts/chickadee.png", "w": 686, "h": 800, "len": 829.0, "beak": [0.78, 0.05, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 37, "b": "AAB+AQAA/8MAAf+DAAP/gwAD/wYAA/8GAAf/PAAH/7wAB//8AA//+AAf//4AH/+/AD//vwA//38Af/98AP//5AD//sAA//3AAf/9gAH//4AD//+AA///gAf//4APg/wADgH8ABwA+AA4APgAeAH4AHAB8ADgA/AAwAeAAMAPAAAAHwAAADwAAAA8AAAAeAAAADAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Downy Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryobates pubescens", "n": 17, "first": "8:52 AM", "last": "9:59 AM", "ts": 1793713952, "mins": [532, 533, 536, 538, 543, 546, 556, 557, 557, 557, 567, 574, 574, 575, 575, 599, 599], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "North America’s smallest woodpecker. It is small enough to forage on thin weed stalks and twigs that bigger woodpeckers can’t cling to.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793112995", "snd": "sounds/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793113092", "sndby": "Thomas G. Graves, xeno-canto XC89855, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "downy", "cm": 16.0, "img": "cutouts/downy.png", "w": 486, "h": 800, "len": 707.0, "beak": [0.59, 0.1, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 53, "b": "AAAAAP/4AAD//AAA//wAAP/8IAD//DwA//wf4H/8D/x//Af+f/wH/3/8A/9//AP/f/wD/3/8B/9//A//f/wf/n/8P/x//H/8f/7//H/+//x////8f////D////x////8f////H////h////4f///+D////A////wP///4D///+A////gP///wD///8A///+AP///gD///wA///4AP//+AD///AA///wAP//sAD//4AA//8AAP//AAD//wAA//